A singly linked list is a chain of nodes. Each node holds a value and a pointer to the next node, and `nil` marks the end:

```go
type Node struct {
	Val  int
	Next *Node
}
head := &Node{Val: 1}
fmt.Println(head.Val, head.Next) // 1 <nil>
```

The list itself is just a `*Node` pointing at the first node. A `nil` head is an empty list. `&Node{Val: v, Next: head}` builds a new node in front of the old head, so prepending is one line and takes constant time:

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

type Node struct {
	Val  int
	Next *Node
}

func main() {
	{{code}}
}
```

```go
var head *Node
head = &Node{Val: 2, Next: head}
head = &Node{Val: 1, Next: head}
fmt.Println(head.Val, head.Next.Val) // 1 2
```

Walk the list by following `Next` until you reach `nil`:

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

type Node struct {
	Val  int
	Next *Node
}

func main() {
	head := &Node{Val: 1, Next: &Node{Val: 2, Next: &Node{Val: 3}}}
	{{code}}
}
```

```go
for n := head; n != nil; n = n.Next {
	fmt.Println(n.Val)
}
```

Unlike a slice, which keeps its elements side by side in one array, every node is a separate allocation, usually on the heap. Walking a list jumps around memory, and reaching item i takes i steps. Never print a node with `fmt.Println(n)` or `%v`: `Next` is an address that changes every run, so print `n.Val`. Go ships a doubly linked list in `container/list`, but building one by hand is how you learn the pointer moves.

example.code:
package main

import "fmt"

type Node struct {
	Val  int
	Next *Node
}

func printList(head *Node) {
	for n := head; n != nil; n = n.Next {
		if n != head {
			fmt.Print(" -> ")
		}
		fmt.Print(n.Val)
	}
	fmt.Println()
}

func main() {
	var nums []int
	for {
		var x int
		if _, err := fmt.Scan(&x); err != nil {
			break
		}
		nums = append(nums, x)
	}

	var head *Node // nil: an empty list
	for i := len(nums) - 1; i >= 0; i-- {
		head = &Node{Val: nums[i], Next: head}
	}

	length, sum := 0, 0
	for n := head; n != nil; n = n.Next {
		length++
		sum += n.Val
	}
	printList(head)
	fmt.Println("length:", length)
	fmt.Println("sum:", sum)
}

